  "name": "sionna_SUTD_two_roof_wander",
  "description": "Two cells on two different roofs, a car on the drop-off road and a pedestrian wandering the open ground between them: the layout that actually exercises the emulator's superposition. multi-gnb-sutd.json splits a pedestrian lap between two cells, and measurement showed the split is total -- at every sampled point at most one cell has a traced path, so every receiver sums exactly one live term and co-channel interference is never produced. The cause was never distance: gnb1 sat 75 m from gnb0 but behind building_2, whose 24.5 m mass blocks it. Here gnb0 keeps building_2 and gnb1 takes building_5, 76 m away across the open ground, each 6 m above its own roof -- 30.5 m and 27.0 m, which differ because the buildings do. Both were picked by sweeping seven real rooftop points against the walk: building_2 is the only one that covers all twelve sampled walk positions AND all twelve car positions, and it beats the UE-to-UE crosstalk at ten of twelve. Moving gnb0 east along building_2 instead, which is where the roof looks clear on the map, put it behind the building's own mass and left it reaching the walk at three of twelve with crosstalk winning ten. ue1's route is a closed wander inside that open ground, generated once from a fixed seed rather than drawn by hand: a point per angular sector, every one at least 6 m clear of every footprint, every leg at least 8 m long and checked against every building. Seeded and not live-random on purpose -- this is a gate, and a failure has to be attributable to the change under test rather than to that run's path.",
